  "result": "I added `click.KeyValue`, a parameter type that parses `key=value` strings into a `(key, value)` tuple. The full test suite passes (1916 passed, 102 skipped, 1 xfailed).\n\nBehaviour:\n- `\"a=b\"` gives `(\"a\", \"b\")`, and `\"a=\"` gives `(\"a\", \"\")`. An empty value is allowed.\n- Only the first `=` splits the string, so `\"a=b=c\"` gives `(\"a\", \"b=c\")`.\n- Input with no `=` or an empty key (`\"ab\"`, `\"=b\"`, `\"\"`) fails with `\"... is not a valid key=value pair.\"`. On the command line this shows up as a usage error with exit code 2.\n- A tuple passed in is returned unchanged.\n\nChanges:\n- `src/click/types.py`: the new `KeyValue` class, placed after `Tuple`.\n- `src/click/__init__.py`: exports `KeyValue`.\n- `tests/test_types.py`: tests for valid and invalid inputs, tuple passthrough, and use with `--set` as a `multiple=True` option.\n- `docs/api.md` and `docs/parameter-types.md`: documentation entries.\n- `CHANGES.md`: a new \"Version 8.6.0 / Unreleased\" entry. The version number is my guess at the next release, so adjust it if needed.\n\nChoices I made that you may want to change:\n- The key is not stripped of whitespace, so `\" a=b\"` gives the key `\" a\"`.\n- The type's display name is `key=value`, which is what appears in error messages.",
